Perhaps I'm nitpicking, but accidentally notepad _is_ a MS Product :p
It also happens to be one of few tools that try to claim that something
in a random two-byte encoding is "plain text".  UTF-16(LE/BE) is not
really used anywhere outside of Microsoft's products, and notepad simply
assumes that everything that starts with 0xFF0xFE (legitimate y-umlaut
and icelandic thorn) to be UTF-16LE.
This also breaks several widely-assumed properties of text files,
including one that you can safely concatenate two of them or append to
one without having to read its contents.

(No, I'm not without guilt myself.  If you use my termrec
(ttyrec-for-Windows), you may have noticed that its recordings include
\e%G\e[8;80;25t (or similar) to note whether you use Unicode or not, and
to record your window size.  These codes will be interpreted or ignored
by some popular terminals, but may result in spurious characters on
other ones, with the only consolation being the fact that NetHack and
most full-screen programs clear the screen as their first action.)

